# Chakra UI Styling Patterns

## Chakra UI v2 Components

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Use Chakra UI components with theme
import { Button, Box, Text, VStack, HStack } from '@chakra-ui/react'

const UserCard = ({ user }: UserCardProps) => {
  return (
    <Box borderWidth="1px" borderRadius="lg" p={4} shadow="md">
      <VStack spacing={3} align="stretch">
        <Text fontSize="xl" fontWeight="bold">
          {user.name}
        </Text>
        <HStack spacing={2}>
          <Button colorScheme="blue" size="sm" onClick={handleEdit}>
            Edit
          </Button>
          <Button variant="outline" size="sm" onClick={handleDelete}>
            Delete
          </Button>
        </HStack>
      </VStack>
    </Box>
  )
}
```

## UI Folder Components - Check First

**Before building custom styled components, check `src/components/ui/` for existing solutions.**

Many common UI patterns are already implemented in the components/ui/ folder:

- GenericCard, MetricCard, EnhancedMetricCard
- StatusBadge, ProgressRing, PriorityIndicator
- Modal, ConfirmationModal
- Input, InputForm, EditableInputForm
- Select, MultiSelect, SearchInput
- Table, ViewSwitcher, PaginationControls
- And more...

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Use existing UI components
import { GenericCard, StatusBadge } from '@/components/ui'

const CaseCard = ({ case }) => {
  return (
    <GenericCard>
      <StatusBadge status={case.status} />
    </GenericCard>
  )
}

// ✅ Good: Create new shared UI component in its own folder
// src/components/ui/PriorityBadge/PriorityBadge.tsx
export const PriorityBadge = ({ priority }) => {
  return (
    <Badge colorScheme={getPriorityColor(priority)}>
      {priority}
    </Badge>
  )
}

// src/components/ui/PriorityBadge/index.ts
export { PriorityBadge } from './PriorityBadge'

// ❌ Bad: Building custom styled versions when UI components exist
// Check components/ui/ folder first before creating new styled components
```

## Chakra Color Tokens - NO Hardcoded Values

**Always use Chakra UI color tokens instead of hardcoded hex values or CSS variables.** This ensures consistency, maintainability, and proper theme support.

### Quick Reference

```typescript
// Semantic text tokens (PREFER THESE)
text.primary      // '#1A202C' (gray.900) - Main body text, active states
text.secondary    // '#4A5568' (gray.700) - Labels, secondary info
text.tertiary     // '#718096' (gray.600) - Less important text
text.muted        // '#A0AEC0' (gray.500) - Muted text (backwards compatibility)
text.disabled     // '#A0AEC0' (gray.500) - Disabled/readonly field values
text.placeholder  // '#CBD5E0' (gray.400) - Input placeholder text

// Raw color tokens (use when semantic tokens don't apply)
gray.100, gray.200, gray.300, gray.600, gray.700
white, black
blue.500, red.500, green.500

// Common conversions
#ffffff → white
#f7f8f9 → gray.100
#e6e6e6 → gray.200
#4a5568 → text.secondary
#A0AEC0 → text.disabled (for readonly/disabled states)
#CBD5E0 → text.placeholder (for input placeholders)
```

### Semantic vs Raw Tokens - Critical Pattern

**ALWAYS prefer semantic tokens (like `text.muted`) over raw color values (like `gray.600`) when they convey the same intent.**

#### ✅ Correct: Use Semantic Tokens

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Semantic tokens for text states
<Input
  value={value}
  isReadOnly
  color="text.disabled"  // ✅ Clearly indicates disabled/readonly state
  placeholder="Enter value..."
  _placeholder={{ color: 'text.placeholder' }}  // ✅ Semantic placeholder
/>

<Text color="text.primary">Active heading</Text>
<Text color="text.secondary">Label text</Text>
<Text color="text.disabled">Disabled field value</Text>

// ✅ GOOD: Component with state-based semantic colors
const EditableField = ({ isEditing, value }) => (
  <Input
    value={value}
    color={isEditing ? "text.primary" : "text.disabled"}  // ✅ Semantic meaning
    placeholder="Type here..."
    _placeholder={{ color: 'text.placeholder' }}
  />
)

// ✅ GOOD: Text component variants
<Text variant="disabled">This field is read-only</Text>
<Text variant="placeholder">Optional hint text</Text>
```

#### ❌ Incorrect: Using Raw Tokens When Semantic Exists

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Using gray.600 when text.tertiary exists
<Text color="gray.600">Less important text</Text>  // Should be text.tertiary

// ❌ BAD: Using gray.500 for disabled text
<Input
  isReadOnly
  color="gray.500"  // ❌ Should be text.disabled
/>

// ❌ BAD: Using gray.400 for placeholder
<Input
  placeholder="Name"
  _placeholder={{ color: 'gray.400' }}  // ❌ Should be text.placeholder
/>

// ❌ BAD: Using gray.900 for main text
<Heading color="gray.900">Title</Heading>  // ❌ Should be text.primary
```

#### When to Use Raw vs Semantic

**Use Semantic Tokens For:**

- Text color in any state (active, disabled, labels, placeholders)
- Component states that have semantic meaning (readonly, disabled, placeholder)
- Input placeholder text (`text.placeholder`)
- Disabled or read-only field values (`text.disabled`)
- Anything that conveys user intent or accessibility

**Use Raw Tokens For:**

- Backgrounds (`gray.100`, `gray.200`)
- Borders (`gray.200`, `gray.300`)
- UI elements without semantic meaning
- Brand colors (`blue.500`, `primary.500`)

### Examples

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Proper token usage
<Box bg="white" borderColor="gray.200">
  <Text color="text.primary">Heading</Text>
  <Button bg="gray.100" color="text.secondary" _hover={{ bg: 'gray.200' }}>
    Click
  </Button>
</Box>

<Input
  bg="white"
  border="1px solid"
  borderColor="gray.200"
  color="text.primary"
  _hover={{ borderColor: 'gray.300' }}
/>

// ❌ BAD: Don't use hex values or CSS variables
<Box bg="#ffffff" borderColor="#e6e6e6">  // DON'T
<Text color="var(--text/primary,#020219)">  // DON'T - use theme tokens
<Input color="gray.500" />  // DON'T - use text.disabled for readonly
<Input _placeholder={{ color: 'gray.400' }} />  // DON'T - use text.placeholder
```

## Verbit UI Library Components

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Use Verbit UI components when available
import { VerbitButton, VerbitCard } from '@verbit-ai/verbit-ui-library'

const DashboardCard = ({ title, children }: DashboardCardProps) => {
  return (
    <VerbitCard title={title} variant="elevated">
      {children}
    </VerbitCard>
  )
}
```

## Theme Usage

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Use Chakra theme values
import { useTheme } from '@chakra-ui/react'

const ThemedComponent = () => {
  const theme = useTheme()

  return (
    <Box
      bg={theme.colors.brand.primary}
      color={theme.colors.white}
      p={theme.space[4]}
    >
      Themed content
    </Box>
  )
}
```

## Responsive Design

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Chakra UI responsive patterns
<Box
  display={{ base: 'block', md: 'flex' }}
  flexDirection={{ base: 'column', md: 'row' }}
  gap={{ base: 4, md: 6 }}
  p={{ base: 4, md: 6, lg: 8 }}
>
  <Box flex={{ md: 1 }}>
    Content
  </Box>
  <Box flex={{ md: 1 }}>
    Sidebar
  </Box>
</Box>
```
